  "query_preview": "..Weak ridging is in place over Utah and southwest\nWyoming this evening bringing dry conditions and relatively light\nflow aloft. A broad upper low is approaching the California coast,\nand a few high clouds are streaming into the area out ahead of it.\nTemperatures today were in the vicinity of 5-7F below seasonal\nnormals for northern Utah and southwest Wyoming, with highs near\nnormal over southern Utah. \n\nWith the California low expected to open up and move into the\nGreat Basin by late Monday, southerly flow will increase over the\narea by Monday evening. This will allow moisture to advect\nnorthward, albeit not as much as we saw last week. The combination\nof that and the position of the low will allow for showers and\nthunderstorms to develop over southwestern portions of the area\novernight Monday into Tuesday morning. The trough is expected to\ncontinue eastward across the area during the day Tuesday as it\nweakens significantly. Scattered to numerous showers and\nthunderstorms are anticipated by Tuesday afternoon, with the best\ncoverage over the higher terrain of central and northern Utah. Any\nstorms that develop will be capable of producing gusty winds,\nlightning, and small hail as well as heavy rain that could lead to\nisolated flash flooding, particularly for prone areas like slot\ncanyons, burn scars, and normally dry washes. \n\nBy Wednesday, most of what's left of the trough will have moved \neast of the area, though most guidance indicates a small piece of\nthe trough lingering off the coast of northern Mexico on Wednesday\nas ridging moves over the top of it, creating a weak Rex Block\npattern. Most guidance then indicates a relatively light or a\nzonal flow over the area for the latter part of the week, keeping\nmost of the area dry with a slow warming trend. It does look like\nthe monsoon hose stays over far southeastern Utah, however,\nkeeping at least some potential of showers and thunderstorms in\nplace. There is some noticeable spread in the guidance, however,\non how far west this hose stays, with some guidance keeping most\nof the moisture over Colorado and far eastern Utah.",
